title: Header::create rejects an all-uppercase age1 recipient
updated: 2026-08-20T14:12:08Z
---

Header.pm:99 dispatches with `if ($recipient =~ /^age1/)` -- case-sensitive, no /i. Its identity counterpart at Header.pm:439 is `$identity =~ /^AGE-SECRET-KEY-1/i` and DOES have /i. So the two halves of the same module disagree.

Consequence, verified end to end: `Keys::decode_public_key(uc $pub)` accepts the key and returns the right bytes, but `Crypt::Age->encrypt(recipients => [uc $pub])` dies with "Unsupported recipient format: AGE1...". Decrypting with an all-lowercase identity works. So the read side keeps the promise, the write side does not.

BIP-173 defines the all-uppercase form as the same valid encoding of the same string, and rage 0.12.1 accepts it. (age 1.2.1 rejects it -- it dispatches on the literal prefix and is stricter than the spec here. That is age deviating, not us.)

Also wrong because of this: .claude/skills/crypt-age-core/SKILL.md, updated in 35e4a03, states flatly that "Keys::decode_* compares the HRP with lc, so it is as permissive as rage". True of decode_* in isolation, false of the encrypt path. The skill text has to be corrected together with the code, or it will mislead the next agent.

Wanted: /i on the recipient dispatch, so the module is symmetric with its own identity half and with its own documentation. NOT the other direction -- removing /i from the identity side would break all-lowercase identities that work today. A mixed-case recipient must keep dying, which it does: it passes the prefix test and then hits the mixed-case guard in bech32_decode.

Found by the release-checker audit, not by the test suite -- t/01-keys.t:99-110 tests decode_public_key(uc(...)) in isolation and never drives it through Header::create, which is exactly the gap that hid this.
